\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If $A\subseteq\mathbb N$ has divergent reciprocal sum, must it contain arithmetic progressions of arbitrarily large finite length with positive common difference?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://people.math.sc.edu/cooper/combprob.html}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
If $A\subseteq\mathbb N$ has divergent reciprocal sum, must it contain arithmetic progressions of arbitrarily large finite length with positive common difference?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 3100048 (AMR-030-0048). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://people.math.sc.edu/cooper/combprob.html}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:3100048}

